To find $h'(x)$, we will need to use the quotient rule *and* the chain rule!

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}h'(x)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\dfrac{u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)}{w(x)}\right]
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)\right]w(x)-u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)w'(x)}{[w(x)]^2}\quad\gray{\text{Quotient rule}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{u'\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)v'(x)w(x)-u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)w'(x)}{[w(x)]^2}\quad\gray{\text{Chain rule}}
\end{align}$

Let's differentiate $u$, $v$, and $w$:

- $u'(x)=-\csc(x)\cot(x)$


- $v'(x)=3$


- $w'(x)=3x^2$

Now we can plug the equations for $u$, $v$, $w$, $u'$, $v'$, *AND* $w'$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{u'\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)}v'(x)w(x)-\maroonC{u\Bigl(v(x)\Bigr)}w'(x)}{[w(x)]^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{-\csc(3x)\cot(3x)}\cdot3\cdot x^3-\maroonC{\csc(3x)}(3x^2)}{(x^3)^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-3x^3\csc(3x)\cot(3x)-3x^2\csc(3x)}{x^6}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-3x^2\csc(3x)\Bigl(x\cot(3x)+1\Bigr)}{x^6}\qquad\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-3\csc(3x)\Bigl(x\cot(3x)+1\Bigr)}{x^4}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel common factors}}
\end{align}$
